# How far a delivery may sit from what it was asked for.
#
# This file says what the drink demands. It does not say what any machine does:
# a band is the same whether the water is heated by a thermoblock, a boiler or
# a fixture that heats nothing, because it is a property of the cup rather than
# of the casting. That is why it is declared here rather than in a plant
# description -- three structures each carrying one would be three statements of
# one fact, free to drift apart, and the tolerance would then vary with the
# machine it was measured on rather than with the drink it exists for.
#
# It carries values, unlike the cadence declaration beside it. Those figures are
# compiled into a loop that runs at one rate on every machine, and their account
# lives here because the value has one home in the source. A band is different:
# it is the criterion trajectories are held against, and what a design costs is
# found out by varying it. A requirement that can only be varied by editing and
# recompiling a source file is one nobody varies, and a claim nobody has tested
# the sensitivity of is a claim nobody has actually established.
#
# Each line is `name = figure unit @kind account`. A band is a half-width: the
# delivery is inside it when what was delivered is no further than this from
# what was commanded, in either direction.
#
# The unit is written on the line rather than assumed. This file once stated
# every band in millidegrees and said so here, which worked for exactly as long
# as temperature was the only thing a delivery was held to; a band in
# thousandths of a millilitre per second cannot be read by a grammar that knows
# only one unit, and a grammar that guessed from the band's name would be
# trusting a spelling nothing checks. So the unit is its own word, drawn from a
# vocabulary the loader holds, and it is checked against the unit this build
# actually keeps that band in -- which is what makes a figure written in the
# wrong quantity a refusal rather than a band a hundred times looser than
# anybody intended. The words are `milli-c` and `milli-ml-s`, declared in
# ../include/delivery_tolerance.h and nowhere else.
#
# Each figure is range-checked as a quantity of its own unit, against a bound
# stated per band rather than one distance shared by all of them. A single
# shared bound would have to be the loosest band's, and would then wave through
# for the tightest one every figure it most needed to refuse.
#
# The vocabulary the kind is drawn from is the one every description in this
# project accounts for its values in, declared in ../include/plant_origin.h and
# nowhere else.
#
# tools/check_control_declaration.py refuses a band that is declared nowhere,
# one that is accounted for with no origin or no account behind its kind, and a
# band that has grown a second home as a definition in the source -- which is
# the failure this file exists to prevent, because a band spelled in two places
# goes on reading as declared here while the software holds deliveries to the
# other one.
#
# What is settled here is that each band is stated, sourced and read. What their
# values should finally be is not: no machine has been on a bench, and the
# figures below are what the design is currently working to rather than what the
# built machine has been shown to hold.

# The window either side of the commanded brew temperature within which the
# water reaching the coffee is still the drink that was ordered.
#
# One degree either side, which is the two-degree span the extraction literature
# treats as the point where a difference in the cup becomes tasteable rather
# than merely measurable. The figure is written as the half-width because that
# is what a band is here, and stating a span in a field read as a half-width
# would declare something twice as loose as the account behind it -- which is
# the way a recorded origin stops supporting its own value.
#
# It is well inside what an unregulated machine of this kind does, which swings
# by considerably more than this across a shot; that is the whole reason a
# control law is being built. It is not the tightest band this design could
# eventually be held to. A machine characterised on a bench may support a
# narrower one, and the honest way to find that out is to narrow this figure and
# see which trajectories stop being accepted.
brew-temperature-band = 1000 milli-c @document Taken from the extraction literature's treatment of two degrees Celsius as the span across which a difference in the cup becomes tasteable rather than only measurable, and recorded here as the half-width that span implies rather than as the span itself. It is a statement about the drink and not a measurement of this machine, which has never been on a bench; what the built machine can actually hold is a bench question and is not claimed here.

# How far the flow a delivery actually moves may sit from the rate its
# profile is commanding before the gap is reported as departure rather than
# absorbed into whatever the delivery ends up producing.
#
# Two hundred thousandths of a millilitre per second either side, which is
# about three per cent of the 7.0 mL/s the reference description declares the
# pump draws at full scale. It is chosen to sit in the gap between two figures:
# above the ripple a vane meter shows on a vibratory pump, so the meter's own
# unsteadiness is not reported as the machine departing from its course; and
# below the shortfall a puck produces when it chokes, so the departure the band
# exists to catch is still caught. Neither of those two figures has been
# measured. No meter has been fitted to a bench and no trajectory has been
# recorded from one, so this is what the design is currently working to rather
# than a figure a built machine has been shown to hold -- the same footing the
# temperature band above is in.
flow-departure-band = 200 milli-ml-s @estimated No meter has been fitted to a bench yet, so this is not read off a recorded trajectory. It is placed between two figures neither of which has been measured: above the ripple a vane meter is expected to show on a vibratory pump, and below the shortfall a choked puck is expected to produce, which puts it at about three per cent of the 7.0 mL/s the reference description declares at full pump scale. It is expected to be revised once a real meter's noise floor and a real pump's step response have been recorded from a bench.
